ole data ","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-22T18:53:11.268722Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-22T18:53:11.269597Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-22T18:53:11.769217Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-22T18:53:11.269565Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-22T18:53:11.768224Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x450 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":23},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n---\n\n### Plot 3.7: Non-Linearity & Bivariate Feature Disconnect\nA scatter plot correlating equipment usage hours (capped at a realistic operational ceiling of 25,000 hours to exclude corrupted inputs) against final sale prices reveals a major modeling hurdle:\n\n* **The Zero-Inflation Spike:** A massive vertical column of data points is concentrated directly on the **0 operational hours** mark, stretching across the entire price span ($7,500 to $140,000). This confirms widespread usage indicator missingness, where fully functional equipment was entered with a zeroed meter by default.\n* **Counter-Intuitive Trend Line:** The linear regression trend line exhibits a flat, slightly positive slope (**0.338**). In asset evaluation, more runtime hours should mathematically decrease valuation due to physical depreciation. The flat positive trend line occurs because the raw signal is heavily distorted by raw size factors—larger, inherently expensive equipment accumulates more total run hours before resale compared to light tools.\n* **Engineering Impact:** A standard linear framework will completely misinterpret this relationship. To fix this interaction trap, tree models require multi-variable interaction support. We must engineer structural interaction features such as **Hours-to-Age Ratio** (`OperationalHoursMeter / MachineAge`) and pass the binary missingness indicator flag (`Hours_isna`) created earlier to help tree splits separate true zero-hour equipment from unrecorded entries.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.8: Pearson Heatmap\ntmp = train.copy()\ntmp['SaleYear']=pd.to_datetime(tmp['TransactionDate'],errors='coerce').dt.year  # coerce this convert to nan of missing data insted of crashing \ntmp['EquipAge']=tmp['SaleYear'] - tmp['ManufactureYear'].where(tmp['ManufactureYear']>1920, np.nan)\ntmp['LogHours']=np.log1p(tmp['OperationalHoursMeter'].clip(upper=50000).fillna(0))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 5.5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.8: Pearson Correlation Heatmap',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\nsns.heatmap(tmp[[TARGET,'EquipAge', 'LogHours', 'SaleYear']].corr(), \n            annot=True,fmt='.3f', cmap='coolwarm', center=0, linewidths=0.5, ax=ax)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-22T18:53:14.318792Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-22T18:53:14.319525Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-22T18:53:14.641253Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-22T18:53:14.31949Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-22T18:53:14.640397Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 700x550 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":24},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Linear Associations & Categorical Operational Demarcation\n\n### Plot 3.8: Linear Correlation Structural Breakdown\nThe Pearson correlation matrix evaluates structural multi-collinearity and isolated linear signal strengths across our engineered numeric feature domain:\n* **The Dominant Negative Vector (`EquipAge`):** Out of all numerical features mapped, `EquipAge` yields the strongest standalone baseline linear association with our target (**-0.352**). This confirms that physical chronological depreciation serves as a fundamental mathematical driver of asset devaluation.\n* **The LogHours Disconnect:** The correlation between the engineered log-transformed runtime variable (`LogHours`) and the target drops to an absolute **-0.000**. While it seems counter-intuitive that usage metric hours show zero linear connection to value, this occurs due to heavy interaction confounding: larger, premium-tier machinery runs for thousands of hours while maintaining high residual values, masking the localized depreciation signal.\n* **Collinearity Observation:** A moderate positive baseline correlation exists between `LogHours` and `SaleYear` (**0.395**). This highlights temporal shifts in data collection—newer transactions are systematically recording operational hours more consistently than legacy ones.\n* **Engineering Impact:** Linear models (like Ridge Regression) will struggle to use `LogHours` effectively due to this zero-correlation mask. However, non-linear ensemble algorithms will successfully unpack this feature by splitting it down branch steps in combination with structural asset dimensions.\n\n---\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.9: Utilization Tier vs Price\ntier_order = ['Low', 'Medium', 'High']\ntier_data = [train[train['UtilizationTier'] == t][TARGET].dropna().values for t in tier_order]\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 4))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.9: Sale Price across Utilization Tiers', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\nbp = ax.boxplot(tier_data, labels=tier_order, patch_artist=True,\n                medianprops=dict(color='black', lw=2),\n                flierprops=dict(marker='.', ms=2, alpha=0.3))\ncolors = ['#27ae60', '#e67e22', '#e74c3c']\nfor patch, c in zip(bp['boxes'], colors):\n    patch.set_facecolor(c)\n    patch.set_alpha(0.7)\nax.set_ylabel(\"TargetValue ($)\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-22T18:53:16.906765Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-22T18:53:16.907116Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-22T18:53:17.114186Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-22T18:53:16.907089Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-22T18:53:17.113405Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x400 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":25},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n### Plot 3.9: Value Stratification Across Utilization Tiers\nEvaluating how final trade valuations map across explicit mechanical `UtilizationTier` classifications outlines a clear, monotonic progression:\n* **The Low Utilization Tier:** Assets categorised under a `Low` operating volume profile map to a lower median market position near `$24,000`, with a tightly packed upper quartile bounding near $40,000.\n\n  \n* **The High Utilization Tier:** Equipment classified as `High` utilization commands a distinctly superior median price point near **$47,000**, shifting the entire interquartile range upward.\n* **The Classification Paradox:** In automotive markets, higher utilization typically implies heavier wear, driving prices *down*. In industrial heavy equipment, however, a \"High\" utilization label indicates premium, heavy-duty production assets (e.g., massive continuous-use mining excavators) that naturally carry significantly larger capital valuations than light support equipment.\n* **Engineering Impact:** The distinct, non-overlapping upward shift in medians proves that `UtilizationTier` provides a powerful predictive sorting signal. Since the category ordering directly maps to an increasing price structure, implementing **Ordinal Encoding** (`Low: 0, Medium: 1, High: 2`) will allow your model to capture this natural value progression perfectly.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.10: Yearly Volume vs. Price Trend\n\nannual_data=train.groupby(tmp['SaleYear'])[TARGET].agg(['median', 'count']).reset_index()\nannual_data=annual_data.rename(columns={'SaleYear': 'Year'})\nfig, ax1=plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 4.5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.10: Yearly Auction Volume vs. Median Price',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\nax2=ax1.twinx()\nax1.bar(annual_data['Year'],annual_data['count'],color='lightsteelblue',alpha=0.5, label='Volume')\na