gUsage_Intensity              72851 non-null   float64       \ndtypes: datetime64[ns](1), float64(7), int32(3), int64(4), object(43)\nmemory usage: 59.8+ MB\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":50},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### 4.1.1 Temporal Feature Extraction\n* **`Trans_Year`**, **`Trans_Month`**, **`Trans_DayOfWeek`**: Extracted from `TransactionDate` to account for macroeconomic trends, annual inflation, and monthly auction volume variations.\n* **`MachineAge`**: Calculated as `Trans_Year - ManufactureYear`. Asset age at transaction time serves as the primary physical driver of value depreciation.\n* **`ManufactureYear_is_placeholder`**: Binary flag indicating records where `ManufactureYear == 1001` prior to median imputation.\n\n#### 4.1.2 Usage Metrics & Non-Linear Intensity Features\n* **`OperationalHoursMeter` (Winsorized)**: Capped at a realistic ceiling of 60,000 hours to eliminate physically impossible values and extreme gradient distortions.\n* **`LogHours`**: Log-transformed meter hours ($\\ln(1 + x)$) to compress right-skewed variance and stabilize linear relationships.\n* **`Usage_Intensity`**: Computed as $\\frac{\\text{OperationalHoursMeter}}{\\text{MachineAge} + 1}$. Measures how aggressively an asset was operated per year of service.\n* **`LogUsage_Intensity`**: Log-transformed usage intensity metric.\n\n#### 4.1.3 Categorical Handling & Column Pruning\n* **Ultra-Sparse Feature Dropping**: Columns missing $>90\\%$ of values (`col18`, `col19`, `col4`, `col5`) are dropped entirely to prevent synthetic imputation noise.\n* **Non-Predictive Column Exclusion**: `TransactionID`, `AssetID`, `TransactionDate`, and `TargetValue` are excluded from model training features.\n* **Sentinel Category Mapping**: Unrecorded string values in categorical features are mapped to a dedicated `'Missing'` sentinel class.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4.2 Validation Strategy — Chronological Split","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n# =========================================================================\n# 4.2.1 RANDOM TRAIN/VAL SPLIT (80/20)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"Creating Random Train/Val Splits (80/20)...\")\n\n# Random 80/20 split on raw features and log target\nX_train_native, X_val_native, y_train, y_val = train_test_split(\n    train_eng[features_raw], y_log, test_size=0.2, random_state=42\n)\nX_test_native = test_eng[features_raw].copy()\n\n\n'''\n# =========================================================================\n# 4.2.2 CHRONOLOGICAL TRAIN/VAL SPLIT (80/20)\n# =========================================================================\n\n# Sort by TransactionDate to get the correct chronological order\nsort_idx = train_eng['TransactionDate'].sort_values().index\nX_sorted = train_eng[features_raw].iloc[sort_idx].copy()\n\ny_sorted = y_log.iloc[sort_idx].copy()\n\nsplit_idx = int(len(X_sorted) * 0.8)\n\n# Split into trainval (80%) and testval (20%)\nX_train_native = X_sorted.iloc[:split_idx].copy()\nX_val_native = X_sorted.iloc[split_idx:].copy()\n\ny_train = y_sorted.iloc[:split_idx].copy()\ny_val = y_sorted.iloc[split_idx:].copy()\n\nX_test_native = test_eng[features_raw].copy() # Final test set\n\n'''","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-22T19:01:45.012123Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-22T19:01:45.012489Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-22T19:01:45.28493Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-22T19:01:45.012459Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-22T19:01:45.28418Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Creating Random Train/Val Splits (80/20)...\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":51,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"\"\\n# =========================================================================\\n# 4.2.2 CHRONOLOGICAL TRAIN/VAL SPLIT (80/20)\\n# =========================================================================\\n\\n# Sort by TransactionDate to get the correct chronological order\\nsort_idx = train_eng['TransactionDate'].sort_values().index\\nX_sorted = train_eng[features_raw].iloc[sort_idx].copy()\\n\\ny_sorted = y_log.iloc[sort_idx].copy()\\n\\nsplit_idx = int(len(X_sorted) * 0.8)\\n\\n# Split into trainval (80%) and testval (20%)\\nX_train_native = X_sorted.iloc[:split_idx].copy()\\nX_val_native = X_sorted.iloc[split_idx:].copy()\\n\\ny_train = y_sorted.iloc[:split_idx].copy()\\ny_val = y_sorted.iloc[split_idx:].copy()\\n\\nX_test_native = test_eng[features_raw].copy() # Final test set\\n\\n\""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":51},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Why NOT Random Split?\nThis dataset represents **historical auction events** sorted by `TransactionDate`. \nA random `train_test_split` causes **future data to leak into training**, producing an \nartificially optimistic local RMSLE (~0.22) that does NOT correlate with the Kaggle public score.\n\n### Chronological Split (Correct Approach)\nWe sort all rows by `TransactionDate` and take:\n* **Train**: First 80% of rows (older auctions)\n* **Validation**: Last 20% of rows (most recent auctions)\n\nThis mirrors the competition setup where the test set contains transactions from **after** the training period.\n\n| Split Type | Local RMSLE | Public RMSLE |\n|---|---|---|\n| Random Split | ~0.22 (misleading) | ~ 0.19702|\n| **Chronological Split** | **~0.27** | **~0.19600** |\n\n> The chronological split local score is a reliable proxy for the public leaderboard.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n## 4.3 OOF TRAGET ENCODING WITH SMOOTHING \n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# =========================================================================\n# 4.3. TARGET ENCODING (OOF & LEAKAGE-FREE)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"Applying Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding...\")\ntarget_enc_cols = ['FunctionalClassification', 'RegionCode']  # MENTION ABOVE HIGH CARDINADALITY FEATURES (TECHNICAL FEATURES)\nglobal_mean = y_train.mean()\nSMOOTH = 25\n\nfor col in target_enc_cols:\n    X_train_native[f'{col}_te'] = np.nan\n    X_val_native[f'{col}_te'] = np.nan\n    X_test_native[f'{col}_te'] = np.nan\n\n    # 1. K-Fold OOF encoding strictly on X_train_native (80%)\n    kf = KFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)\n    for t_idx, v_idx in kf.split(X_train_native):\n        stats = pd.DataFrame({\n            'cat': X_train_native[col].iloc[t_idx].fillna('missing'),\n            'target': y_train.iloc[t_idx]\n        })\n        agg = stats.groupby('cat')['target'].agg(['mean', 'count'])\n        smoothed_map = (agg['mean'] * agg['count'] + global_mean * SMOOTH) / (agg['count'] + SMOOTH)\n\n        X_train_native.loc[X_train_native.index[v_idx], f'{col}_te'] = X_train_native[col].iloc[v_idx].fillna('missing').map(smoothed_map)\n\n    # 2. Map full  X_val_native averages to testval (20%) and final test\n    stats_full = pd.DataFrame({\n        'cat': X_train_native[col].fillna('missing'),\n        'target': y_train\n    })\n    agg_full = stats_full.groupby('cat')['target'].agg(['mean', 'count'])\n    full_smoothed_map = (agg_full['mean'] * agg_full['count'] + global_mean * SMOOTH) / (agg_full['count'] + SMOOTH)\n\n    X_train_native[f'{col}_te'] = X_train_native[f'{col}_te'].fillna(global_mean)\n    X_val_native[f'{col}_te'] = X_val_native[col].fillna('missing').map(full_smoothed_map).fillna(global_mean)\n    X_test_native[f'{col}_te'] = X_test_native[col].fillna('missing').map(full_smoothed_map).fillna(global_mean)\n\n# Updated features list\nfeatures_updated = X_train_native.columns.tolist()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-22T19:02:16.249465Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-22T19:02:16.250106Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-22T19:02:16.598191Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-22T19:02:16.250067Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-22T19:02:16.597345Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Applying Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding...\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":52},{"cell_type":"code","source":"X_train_native.info()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-22T18:55:36.9497Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-22T18:55:36.950741Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-22T18:55:37.14421Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-22T18:55:36.950702Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-22T18:55:37.143342Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"<class 'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame'>\nIndex: 110960 entries, 35835 to 121958\nData columns (total 49 columns):\n #   Column                          Non-Null Count   Dtype  \n---  ------                          --------------   -----  \n 0   ProductConfigID                 110960 non-null  int64  \n 1   DataOriginCode                  110960 non-null  object \n 2   VendorPartnerID                 110960 non-null  object \n 3   UtilizationTier                 40395 non-null   object \n 4   Spec_FullDescriptor             110960 non-null  object \n 5   Spec_BaseClass                  110960 non-null  object \n 6   Spec_SubClass                   81326 non-null   object \n 7   Spec_ReleaseSeries              26351 non-null   object \n 8   Spec_VariantModifier            41929 non-null   object \n 9   AssetScaleFactor                78020 non-null   object \n 10  FunctionalClassification        110960 non-null  object \n 11  RegionCode                      110960 non-null  object \n 12  InventoryGroupCategory          110960 non-null  object \n 13  InventoryGroupDescription       110960 non-null  object \n 14  col1                            27016 non-null   object \n 15  CabinType                       110960 non-null  object \n 16  Forks                           23454 non-null   object \n 17  col3                            23402 non-null   object \n 18  DrivetrainType                  39025 non-null   object \n 19  col6                            18698 non-null   object \n 20  col7                            18698 non-null   object \n 21  col8                            18698 non-null   object \n 22  col9                            18698 non-null   object \n 23  col10                           102642 non-null  object \n 24  col11                           18698 non-null   object \n 25  col12                           30707 non-null   object \n 26  col13                           18698 non-null   object \n 27  col14                           18698 non-null   object \n 28  col15                           33782 non-null   object \n 29  col16                           71935 non-null   object \n 30  col20                           56797 non-null   object \n 31  col21                           56797 non-null   object \n 32  col22                           56799 non-null   object \n 33  col23                           56799 non-null   object \n 34  col24                           56799 non-null   object \n 35  col25                           56797 non-null   object \n 36  col27                           12009 non-null   object \n 37  col28                           12009 non-null   object \n 38  col29                           15084 non-null   object \n 39  col30                           15084 non-null   object \n 40  Trans_Year                      110960 non-null  int32  \n 41  Trans_Month                     110960 non-null  int32  \n 42  Trans_DayOfWeek                 110960 non-null  int32  \n 43  ManufactureYear_is_placeholder  110960 non-null  int64  \n 44  MachineAge                      99201 non-null   float64\n 45  LogHours                        65706 non-null   float64\n 46  LogUsage_Intensity              58368 non-null   float64\n 47  FunctionalClassification_te     110960 non-null  float64\n 48  RegionCode_te                   110960 non-null  float64\ndtypes: float64(5), int32(3), int64(2), object(39)\nmemory usage: 45.1+ MB\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":34},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 4. Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding with Smoothing\n\n#### **Concept & Motivation**\nCategorical features with high cardinality (such as `FunctionalClassification` and `RegionCode`) can create excessively sparse matrices when using standard One-Hot Encoding. **Target Encoding** resolves this by replacing each categorical level with the expected mean of the target variable (`TargetValue`).\n\nHowever, native target encoding introduces two significant risks:\n1. **Data Leakage & Overfitting:** Directly encoding training samples using their own target values leads to target leakage.\n2. **Rare Category Noise:** Categories with very few instances (e.g., 1 or 2 auction records) produce extreme, unrepresentative target averages.\n\n---\n\n#### **Methodology & Mathematical Formulation**\n\nTo eliminate data leakage and stabilize rare category estimates, we combine **K-Fold Out-of-Fold (OOF) Partitioning** with **Empirical Bayes (M-estimate) Smoothing**:\n\n$$\\hat{S}_{\\text{cat}} = \\frac{\\bar{y}_{\\text{cat}} \\cdot N_{\\text{cat}} + \\bar{y}_{\\text{global}} \\cdot m}{N_{\\text{cat}} + m}$$\n\nWhere:\n* $\\bar{y}_{\\text{cat}}$ = Category target mean within the training fold.\n* $N_{\\text{cat}}$ = Total observation count for the category in the fold.\n* $\\bar{y}_{\\text{global}}$ = Global mean target across the full training fold.\n* $m$ = Smoothing weight parameter ($\\text{SMOOTH} = 25$).\n\nThe mathematical formula used in your code is:$$\\text{Smoothed Value} = \\frac{(\\text{Category Mean} \\times \\text{Count}) + (\\text{Global Mean} \\times \\text{SMOOTH})}{\\text{Count} + \\text{SMOOTH}}$$\n---\n\n#### **Pipeline Execution Steps**\n1. **OOF Split:** Divide `X_trainval` into 5 stratified/shuffled folds.\n2. **Validation Encoding:** For each fold, compute the smoothed map strictly on the remaining 4 folds ($80\\%$) and apply it to the holdout fold ($20\\%$).\n3. **Test Mapping:** Compute a global smoothed map over the complete $100\\%$ `X_trainval` distribution and project it onto `X_testval` and `X_test`.\n4. **Fallback Handling:** Any unseen or `NaN` categories are dynamically imputed with the global mean ($\\bar{y}_{\\text{global}}$).\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":" ### 4.3 Target Encoding (Out-of-Fold)\nHigh-cardinality categoricals (`ProductConfigID`, `Spec_BaseClass`, `RegionCode`, `VendorPartnerID`, `FunctionalClassification`) are encoded using **Out-of-Fold Target Encoding** to prevent target leakage:\n* Train fold: 5-Fold OOF mean of `log1p(TargetValue)`\n* Validation/Test: static lookup from training statistics\n\n\n* **The Leakage Prevention Loop:** The training target means are computed purely on isolated internal training splits (`train_idx`), then projected outward onto the validation subset (`val_idx`). This mirrors true inference constraints because the encoding matrix does not integrate information from the data points it is evaluating.\n* **Target Scale Alignment:** The categorical target means are calculated directly against the log-transformed target space (`y_log`). This aligns the newly generated continuous features perfectly with the core model optimization metric (RMSLE).\n* **Global Generalization Fallback:** Unseen categories or missing values within test splits are safely mapped to the overall `y_log.mean()`, ensuring the model pipeline does not introduce missing value states into downstream tree architectures.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4.3 Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding with Smoothing\n\n### Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding Rationale\n\n#### **High-Cardinality Categorical Encoding**\nHigh-cardinality features such as `FunctionalClassification` (hundreds of categories) and `RegionCode` (53 regions) create extremely sparse matrices under standard One-Hot Encoding. Target encoding replaces category strings with target statistics, but doing so directly on training data introduces severe **target leakage**.\n\n#### **5-Fold OOF Scheme with Bayesian Smoothing**\nTo ensure complete leakage prevention:\n1. **Out-of-Fold Split**: Target statistics for each training row are calculated strictly from the remaining 4 folds.\n2. **Bayesian Smoothing ($\\text{SMOOTH} = 25$)**: Small-sample categories are shrunk toward the global log-target mean $y_{\\text{global}}$:\n   $$\\text{TE}(c) = \\frac{n_c \\cdot \\bar{y}_c + m \\cdot y_{\\text{global}}}{n_c + m}$$\n   where $n_c$ is the category count, $\\bar{y}_c$ is the category mean target, and $m = 25$ is the smoothing weight.\n\n> **Key Takeaway**: Target Encoding transforms high-cardinality nominal variables into smooth, continuous conditional expectations while protecting against target leakage.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"##  4.4 Scaling & Imputation","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n\n# =========================================================================\n# 4.4. DUAL-TRACK PREPROCESSING (SCALING & ENCODING WITH ALIGNED CATEGORIES)\n# =========================================================================\n\nprint(\"Setting up Preprocessing Tracks...\")\n\n# Track A: Prepare and ALIGN Native Categoricals for LightGBM and XGBoost\n\nfor col in features_updated:\n    if X_train_native[col].dtype == 'object' or not pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(X_train_native[col]):\n        # Find union of all categories across Train, Val, and Test\n        \n        train_vals = set(X_train_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        val_vals = set(X_val_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        test_vals = set(X_test_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        all_cats = list(train_vals.union(val_vals).union(test_vals))\n\n        # Convert to pd.Categorical using the exact same categories set\n        X_train_native[col] = pd.Categorical(X_train_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n        X_val_native[col] = pd.Categorical(X_val_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n        X_test_native[col] = pd.Categorical(X_test_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n\n\n# Track B: Imputation & Scaling Pipeline for Scikit-learn Baselines\nnum_feats = X_train_native.select_dtypes(include=[np.number]).columns.tolist()\ncat_feats = X_train_native.select_dtypes(include=['category']).columns.tolist()\n\nnum_pipe = Pipeline([\n    ('impute', SimpleImputer(strategy='median')),\n    ('scale',  StandardScaler())\n])\ncat_pipe = Pipeline([\n    ('fill',   SimpleImputer(strategy='constant', fill_value='Unknown')),\n    ('encode', OrdinalEncoder(handle_unknown='use_encoded_value', unknown_value=-1))\n])\n\npreproc = ColumnTransformer([\n    ('num', num_pipe, num_feats),\n    ('cat', cat_pipe, cat_feats)\n], remainder='drop')\n\n# fit_transform on trainval, transform on X_val_native and test\nX_train_proc = preproc.fit_transform(X_train_native)\nX_val_proc = preproc.transform(X_val_native)\nX_test_proc = preproc.transform(X_test_native)\n\nprint(f\"Track A Native Shape: {X_train_native.shape}\")\nprint(f\"Track B Processed Shape: {X_train_proc.shape}\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-22T18:55:49.109303Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-22T18:55:49.11023Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-22T18:55:54.298027Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-22T18:55:49.110197Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-22T18:55:54.297235Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Setting up Preprocessing Tracks...\nTrack A Native Shape: (110960, 49)\nTrack B Processed Shape: (110960, 49)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":35},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4.4 Dual-Track Preprocessing Architecture\n\nTo optimize performance across diverse model architectures, we maintain two parallel preprocessing tracks:\n* **Track A (Native Categorical Track)**: Retains pandas categorical types and raw numerical columns for gradient boosted decision trees (`LightGBM` and `XGBoost`).\n* **Track B (Imputed & Scaled Track)**: Applies `SimpleImputer` (median/mode) and `StandardScaler` to generate dense numeric matrices for distance-based, linear, and neural models (`Ridge Regression`, `Random Forest`, `Extra Trees`, `MLP`).\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 5. Model Training\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# =========================================================================\n# 5. BASELINE MODELS EVALUATION (TRACK B)\n# =========================================================================\n\nmodels = {\n    'Ridge Regression': Ridge(alpha=10.0),\n    'Random Forest': RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, max_depth=12, min_samples_leaf=10, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1),\n    'Extra Trees': ExtraTreesRegressor(n_estimators=100, max_depth=12, min_samples_leaf=10, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1),\n    'XGBoost': xgb.XGBRegressor(n_estimators=500, learning_rate=0.05, max_depth=7, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.7, tree_method='hist', random_state=42, verbosity=0),\n    'LightGBM': lgb.LGBMRegressor(n_estimators=500, learning_rate=0.05, num_leaves=63, max_depth=8, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.7, random_state=42, verbose=-1, n_jobs=-1),\n\n}\n\nresults = {}\nfor name, model in models.items():\n    print(f\"Training {name}...\")\n    model.fit(X_train_proc, y_train)\n    preds = model.predict(X_val_proc)\n    rmsle_val = root_mean_squared_error(y_val, preds)\n    results[name] = rmsle_val\n    print(f\"  -> Val RMSLE: {rmsle_val:.5f}\")\n\ncomparison_df = pd.DataFrame(list(results.items()), columns=['Model', 'Val_RMSLE']).sort_values('Val_RMSLE')\nprint(\"\\n--- Model Performance Comparison Table ---\")\nprint(comparison_df.to_string(index=False))\n\n# Plotting performance\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 4.5))\nfig.suptitle('Baseline Models Val RMSLE Comparison', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\nbars = ax.bar(comparison_df['Model'], comparison_df['Val_RMSLE'], color=sns.color_palette('viridis', len(comparison_df)), edgec