': 0.05, 'max_depth': 6, 'subsample': 0.9}","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 6.1 Automated Hyperparameter Tuning Breakdown (GridSearchCV)\n\nThis section performs automated hyperparameter tuning for **LightGBM** and **XGBoost** models using a fast, sampled 30,000-row subset evaluated on a leak-free `PredefinedSplit`.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Code Execution Workflow\n\n1. **Data Concatenation (`X_search_native`, `y_search`)**: Combines training and validation splits into a single unified search dataset.\n2. **Categorical Dtype Casting**: Converts string/object columns to pandas `category` dtype, allowing LightGBM and XGBoost to perform **native categorical splits** without One-Hot Encoding overhead.\n3. **30,000 Sample Limit (Speed Optimization)**: Randomly samples 30,000 rows (`tuning_sample_size`) to execute grid search quickly without running out of RAM.\n4. **Leak-Free Predefined Split (`PredefinedSplit`)**: Rebuilds exact training (`-1`) and validation (`0`) fold indices, forcing `GridSearchCV` to evaluate parameters strictly on the validation set.\n5. **Exhaustive Grid Search (`GridSearchCV`)**: Evaluates all 8 parameter combinations for LightGBM and 8 combinations for XGBoost using `scoring='neg_root_mean_squared_error'`.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Hyperparameter Impact Analysis\n\n### A. LightGBM Hyperparameters (`LGBMRegressor`)\n\n| Hyperparameter | Values Tested | Function & Mechanism | Model Impact & Trade-offs |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **`num_leaves`** | `[63, 95]` | Controls the maximum number of leaves per tree. | **Primary complexity knob**: Higher values allow deeper leaf-wise fitting, but increase risk of **overfitting**. |\n| **`max_depth`** | `[8, 10]` | Limits the vertical height of any tree branch. | Prevents unbalanced tree branches from growing excessively deep when `num_leaves` is high. |\n| **`learning_rate`** | `[0.03, 0.05]` | Step-size shrinkage weight ($\\eta$) for updates. | Lower values (0.03) slow down updates, requiring more trees but improving test generalization. |\n| **`n_estimators`** | `300` | Total number of boosting iterations (trees). | Controls overall model capacity. |\n| **`subsample`** | `0.8` | Row sampling ratio per tree (Bagging). | Randomly samples 80% of rows per tree, reducing variance and preventing memorization. |\n| **`colsample_bytree`** | `0.7` | Feature sampling ratio per tree split. | Randomly selects 70% of features per tree, decorrelating individual trees in the ensemble. |\n\n---\n\n### B. XGBoost Hyperparameters (`XGBRegressor`)\n\n| Hyperparameter | Values Tested | Function & Mechanism | Model Impact & Trade-offs |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **`max_depth`** | `[6, 8]` | Maximum depth of each level-wise tree. | **Primary complexity knob**: Higher values (8) create complex trees capturing high-order interactions. |\n| **`subsample`** | `[0.7, 0.9]` | Fraction of training instances sampled per tree. | Subsamples training rows per tree to reduce model variance and prevent overfitting. |\n| **`learning_rate`** | `[0.03, 0.05]` | Shrinkage applied to new tree feature weights. | Smaller values (0.03) prevent overshooting the optimal loss minimum during optimization. |\n| **`colsample_bytree`** | `0.7` | Column subsampling ratio per tree split. | Forces XGBoost to explore secondary feature interactions by masking 30% of features per tree. |\n| **`tree_method`** | `'hist'` | Histogram-based continuous feature binning. | **Performance optimization**: Groups continuous values into discrete bins for fast tree building. |\n| **`enable_categorical`** | `True` | Native categorical feature support. | Enables direct splitting on `category` dtypes without requiring One-Hot Encoding. |\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"'''\n# =========================================================================\n# 6.2. HYPERPARAMETER TUNING (LIGHTGBM & XGBOOST ONLY VIA RandomizedSearchCV)\n# =========================================================================\n\nprint(\"--- Automated Hyperparameter Tuning using RandomizedSearchCV ---\")\n\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import PredefinedSplit, RandomizedSearchCV\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\n# =========================================================================\n# 0. Setup Predefined Split for Chronological Validation (prevents leakage)\n# =========================================================================\n# Combine train and val for the search API\nX_search_native = pd.concat([X_train_native, X_val_native]).copy()\nX_search_proc = np.vstack([X_train_proc, X_val_proc])\ny_search = pd.concat([y_train, y_val])\n\n# CRITICAL FIX: Cast object/non-numeric columns to categorical for LightGBM & XGBoost\nfor col in X_search_native.columns:\n    if X_search_native[col].dtype == 'object' or not pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(X_search_native[col]):\n        X_search_native[col] = X_search_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing').astype('category')\n\n# Create split index: -1 for train fold, 0 for validation fold\nsplit_index = [-1] * len(X_train_native) + [0] * len(X_val_native)\npds = PredefinedSplit(test_fold=split_index)\n\n# =========================================================================\n# 1. LightGBM Tuning (Track A - Native Categoricals)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"\\nTuning Model 1: LightGBM (Native Categoricals)...\")\n\nlgb_param_dist = {\n    'num_leaves': [31, 63, 127],\n    'max_depth': [6, 8, 10],\n    'learning_rate': [0.03, 0.05],\n    'subsample': [0.7, 0.85],\n    'colsample_bytree': [0.6, 0.8]\n}\n\nlgb_estimator = lgb.LGBMRegressor(n_estimators=1000, random_state=42, verbose=-1, n_jobs=-1)\n\nlgb_search = RandomizedSearchCV(\n    estimator=lgb_estimator,\n    param_distributions=lgb_param_dist,\n    n_iter=6,  # Number of parameter combinations to sample\n    scoring='neg_root_mean_squared_error',\n    cv=pds,\n    random_state=42,\n    n_jobs=-1\n)\n\nlgb_search.fit(X_search_native, y_search)\nbest_lgb_params = lgb_search.best_params_\nprint(f\"Best LightGBM Parameters: {best_lgb_params}\")\nprint(f\"Best LightGBM Val RMSLE:  {-lgb_search.best_score_:.5f}\")\n\n# =========================================================================\n# 2. XGBoost Tuning (Track A - Native Categoricals)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"\\nTuning Model 2: XGBoost (Native Categoricals)...\")\n\nxgb_param_dist = {\n    'max_depth': [6, 8, 10],\n    'learning_rate': [0.03, 0.05],\n    'subsample': [0.7, 0.9],\n    'colsample_bytree': [0.6, 0.8]\n}\n\nxgb_estimator = xgb.XGBRegressor(\n    n_estimators=800, \n    tree_method='hist', \n    enable_categorical=True, \n    random_state=42, \n    verbosity=0, \n    n_jobs=-1\n)\n\nxgb_search = RandomizedSearchCV(\n    estimator=xgb_estimator,\n    param_distributions=xgb_param_dist,\n    n_iter=6,\n    scoring='neg_root_mean_squared_error',\n    cv=pds,\n    random_state=42,\n    n_jobs=-1\n)\n\nxgb_search.fit(X_search_native, y_search)\nbest_xgb_params = xgb_search.best_params_\nprint(f\"Best XGBoost Parameters: {best_xgb_params}\")\nprint(f\"Best XGBoost Val RMSLE:  {-xgb_search.best_score_:.5f}\")\n\n# =========================================================================\n# 3. CatBoost Tuning (Track B - Processed Data)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"\\nTuning Model 3: CatBoost (Processed Data)...\")\n\ncat_param_dist = {\n    'depth': [6, 8],\n    'l2_leaf_reg': [3, 5, 7],\n    'learning_rate': [0.03, 0.05]\n}\n\ncat_estimator = CatBoostRegressor(iterations=1000, loss_function='RMSE', random_seed=42, verbose=0)\n\ncat_search = RandomizedSearchCV(\n    estimator=cat_estimator,\n    param_distributions=cat_param_dist,\n    n_iter=4,\n    scoring='neg_root_mean_squared_error',\n    cv=pds,\n    random_state=42,\n    n_jobs=-1\n)\n\ncat_search.fit(X_search_proc, y_search)\nbest_cat_params = cat_search.best_params_\nprint(f\"Best CatBoost Parameters: {best_cat_params}\")\nprint(f\"Best CatBoost Val RMSLE:  {-cat_search.best_score_:.5f}\")\n'''","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#  on corongical spilit\n\n'''\nHyperparameter Tuning using RandomizedSearchCV\n\nTuning Model 1: LightGBM (Native Categoricals)...\nBest LightGBM Parameters: {'subsample': 0.7, 'num_leaves': 127, 'max_depth': 8, 'learning_rate': 0.05, 'colsample_bytree': 0.6}\nBest LightGBM Val RMSLE:  0.26473\n\nTuning Model 2: XGBoost (Native Categoricals)...\nBest XGBoost Parameters: {'subsample': 0.9, 'max_depth': 8, 'learning_rate': 0.05, 'colsample_bytree': 0.6}\nBest XGBoost Val RMSLE:  0.26526\n\nTuning Model 3: CatBoost (Processed Data)...\nBest CatBoost Parameters: {'learning_rate': 0.05, 'l2_leaf_reg': 5, 'depth': 8}\nBest CatBoost Val RMSLE:  0.27929\n this took around 1hr 30 min\n'''\n'''\n\n#parameters based on RandomizedSearchCV results\nbest_lgb_params = {\n    \"subsample\": 0.7,\n    \"num_leaves\": 127,\n    \"max_depth\": 8,\n    \"learning_rate\": 0.05,\n    \"colsample_bytree\": 0.6,\n}\n\nbest_xgb_params = {\n    \"subsample\": 0.9,\n    \"max_depth\": 8,\n    \"learning_rate\": 0.05,\n    \"colsample_bytree\": 0.6,\n}\n'''\n#best_cat_params = {\"learning_rate\": 0.05, \"l2_leaf_reg\": 5, \"depth\": 8}","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 6.2 Automated Hyperparameter Tuning Breakdown (RandomizedSearchCV)\n\nThis section performs automated hyperparameter search for **LightGBM**, **XGBoost**, and **CatBoost** using `RandomizedSearchCV` on the full dataset, evaluated against the leak-free chronological `PredefinedSplit`.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Code Execution Workflow\n\n1. **Dual-Track Search Datasets**:\n   * **Track A (Native Categoricals)**: Combines `X_train_native` and `X_val_native` into `X_search_native` for LightGBM and XGBoost, casting non-numeric features to `category` dtypes.\n   * **Track B (Processed Data)**: Stacks `X_train_proc` and `X_val_proc` into dense matrix `X_search_proc` for CatBoost.\n2. **Chronological Predefined Split (`PredefinedSplit`)**:\n   Assigns `-1` to all historical training rows ($N_{\\text{train}}$) and `0` to validation rows ($N_{\\text{val}}$). Forces `RandomizedSearchCV` to score configurations strictly on future validation transactions.\n3. **Stochastic Sampling (`n_iter`)**:\n   Instead of testing every combination (which would take hours), `RandomizedSearchCV` randomly samples a fixed number of parameter combinations (`n_iter=6` for LGBM/XGB, `n_iter=4` for CatBoost) to find near-optimal parameters efficiently.\n4. **Validation RMSLE Scoring**:\n   Evaluates each candidate model using `scoring='neg_root_mean_squared_error'` ($\\text{RMSLE} = -\\text{score}$).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Hyperparameter Impact Analysis\n\n### A. LightGBM Hyperparameters (`LGBMRegressor`)\n\n| Hyperparameter | Values Sampled | Function & Mechanism | Model Impact & Trade-offs |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **`num_leaves`** | `[31, 63, 127]` | Maximum number of leaf nodes per tree. | Controls tree capacity. High values (127) fit complex patterns but increase overfitting risk. |\n| **`max_depth`** | `[6, 8, 10]` | Max vertical tree depth limit. | Constrains leaf-wise growth to prevent individual deep overfitted branches. |\n| **`learning_rate`** | `[0.03, 0.05]` | Shrinkage step size ($\\eta$) per update. | Smaller values (0.03) slow updates, improving generalization with $1000$ trees. |\n| **`subsample`** | `[0.7, 0.85]` | Row sampling ratio per tree (Bagging). | Subsamples 70%–85% of training rows per tree to reduce model variance. |\n| **`colsample_bytree`** | `[0.6, 0.8]` | Feature sampling ratio per tree split. | Masks 20%–40% of features per tree split, forcing exploration of secondary signals. |\n\n---\n\n### B. XGBoost Hyperparameters (`XGBRegressor`)\n\n| Hyperparameter | Values Sampled | Function & Mechanism | Model Impact & Trade-offs |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **`max_depth`** | `[6, 8, 10]` | Max level-wise depth of each tree. | Controls tree depth. Level 8–10 trees capture deep high-order interactions. |\n| **`learning_rate`** | `[0.03, 0.05]` | Step shrinkage rate ($\\eta$). | Stabilizes gradient updates when training $800$ histogram trees. |\n| **`subsample`** | `[0.7, 0.9]` | Row sampling ratio per tree. | Randomly samples training instances to prevent memorizing specific rows. |\n| **`colsample_bytree`** | `[0.6, 0.8]` | Feature sampling ratio per tree split. | Subsamples features per split to decorrelate individual trees in the ensemble. |\n\n---\n\n### C. CatBoost Hyperparameters (`CatBoostRegressor`)\n\n| Hyperparameter | Values Sampled | Function & Mechanism | Model Impact & Trade-offs |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **`depth`** | `[6, 8]` | Depth of symmetric decision trees. | CatBoost builds **oblivious trees** (same split at each level). Depth 8 balances speed and complexity. |\n| **`l2_leaf_reg`** | `[3, 5, 7]` | Coefficient of L2 regularization term. | **Overfitting penalty**: Higher L2 regularization values (5–7) penalize large leaf weights, smoothing predictions. |\n| **`learning_rate`** | `[0.03, 0.05]` | Gradient step shrinkage rate. | Controls update step size over $1000$ boosting iterations. |\n| **`iterations`** | `1000` | Total number of trees built. | Controls total boosting capacity on processed numeric data. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Takeaways & Comparison\n\n* **GridSearchCV vs. RandomizedSearchCV**: `GridSearchCV` evaluates every point on a fixed grid; `RandomizedSearchCV` samples random parameter space combinations, allowing you to explore larger parameter ranges (`n_estimators=1000`, `num_leaves=127`) in a fraction of the time.\n* **Regularization**: `subsample`, `colsample_bytree`, and CatBoost's `l2_leaf_reg` work together to prevent deep trees (`max_depth=8/10`) from memorizing noisy training records.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 7.Submission","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# =========================================================================\n# 7.1. GENERATING FINAL SUBMISSIONS (TRAINED ON 100% DATA WITH ALIGNED CATS)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"\\n--- Training Final LightGBM & XGBoost on 100% data ---\")\n\nX_native_full = train_eng[features_raw].copy()\nX_test_native_full = test_eng[features_raw].copy()\n\n# Add target-encoded columns using full train set statistics\nfor col in target_enc_cols:\n    stats_full = pd.DataFrame({\n        'cat': train_eng[col].fillna('missing'),\n        'target': y_log\n    })\n    agg_full = stats_full.groupby('cat')['target'].agg(['mean', 'count'])\n    full_smoothed_map = (agg_full['mean'] * agg_full['count'] + y_log.mean() * SMOOTH) / (agg_full['count'] + SMOOTH)\n\n    X_native_full[f'{col}_te'] = train_eng[col].fillna('missing').map(full_smoothed_map).fillna(y_log.mean())\n    X_test_native_full[f'{col}_te'] = test_eng[col].fillna('missing').map(full_smoothed_map).fillna(y_log.mean())\n\n# ALIGN CATEGORIES: Find union of all categories between full train and test sets to prevent XGBoostError\nfor col in X_native_full.columns:\n    if X_native_full[col].dtype == 'object' or not pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(X_native_full[col]):\n        train_vals = set(X_native_full[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        test_vals = set(X_test_native_full[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        all_cats = list(train_vals.union(test_vals))\n\n        # Convert both columns to Categorical sharing the exact same list of categories\n        X_native_full[col] = pd.Categorical(X_native_full[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n        X_test_native_full[col] = pd.Categorical(X_test_native_full[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.1 Retraining Data Preparation & Category Alignment\n\nThis section prepares 100% of the training dataset for final model retraining and addresses critical categorical alignment challenges between train and test datasets.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Code Execution Workflow\n\n1. **100% Training Matrix Assembly (`X_native_full`, `X_test_native_full`)**:\n   Transitioning from validation to production: We concatenate all training rows to train final models on 100% of available labeled data, maximizing historical signal before predicting on un-labeled test transactions.\n2. **Full Dataset Target Encoding**:\n   Recalculates smooth target encoding maps across the entire target vector (`y_log`) using Bayesian smoothing ($\\text{SMOOTH} = 25$) for high-cardinality features (`FunctionalClassification` and `RegionCode`):\n   $$\\text{TE}_{\\text{full}}(c) = \\frac{n_c \\cdot \\bar{y}_c + m \\cdot y_{\\text{global}}}{n_c + m}$$\n   Maps fallback values to the global mean $y_{\\text{global}}$ for any category present only in the test set.\n3. **Category Union Alignment (`all_cats`)**:\n   Enforces identical categorical dtype structures across train and test sets to guarantee seamless inference.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Deep-Dive: Why Category Alignment is Critical\n\n### **The Problem: Categorical Mismatch Errors**\nWhen training gradient boosted models (`XGBoost` with `enable_categorical=True` or `LightGBM`) on native pandas `category` dtypes:\n* If the test dataset contains unseen category labels, or\n* If pandas assigns different internal integer index codes to categories (e.g., `'Florida'` is index `2` in train but index `4` in test),\n\nXGBoost and LightGBM will throw fatal runtime mismatch errors or produce corrupted predictions:\n$$\\text{XGBoostError: Categorical categories mismatch between train and test feature matrices.}$$\n\n### **The Solution: Category Set Union**\n1. Computes the mathematical **union** of all unique string labels present across both train and test columns:\n   $$\\mathcal{C}_{\\text{all}} = \\mathcal{C}_{\\text{train}} \\cup \\mathcal{C}_{\\text{test}}$$\n2. Casts both columns using pandas `pd.Categorical(..., categories=all_cats)`.\n3. Ensures every category string maps to the **exact same internal integer code** across both training and testing matrices, ensuring $100\\%$ safe prediction pipelines.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 1. LightGBM\nprint(\"Training final LightGBM...\")\nlgb_model = lgb.LGBMRegressor(\n    n_estimators=1200,\n    subsample=0.8,\n    colsample_bytree=0.7,\n    random_state=42,\n    verbose=-1,\n    n_jobs=-1,\n    **best_lgb_params\n)\nlgb_model.fit(X_native_full, y_log)\nlgb_test_preds = lgb_model.predict(X_test_native_full)\n\n# Save pure LightGBM submission\nlgb_raw = np.expm1(lgb_test_preds).clip(0, None)\n#pd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': lgb_raw}).to_csv('submission_lgbm.csv', index=False)\n#print(\"Saved submission_lgbm.csv\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 2. XGBoost\nprint(\"Training final XGBoost...\")\nxgb_model = xgb.XGBRegressor(\n    n_estimators=1000,\n    colsample_bytree=0.7,\n    tree_method='hist',\n    enable_categorical=True,\n    random_state=42,\n    verbosity=0,\n    n_jobs=-1,\n    **best_xgb_params\n)\nxgb_model.fit(X_native_full, y_log)\nxgb_test_preds = xgb_model.predict(X_test_native_full)\n\n# Save pure XGBoost submission\nxgb_raw = np.expm1(xgb_test_preds).clip(0, None)\n#pd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': xgb_raw}).to_csv('submission_xgb.csv', index=False)\n#print(\"Saved submission_xgb.csv\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# =========================================================================\n# 7.2. GENERATING BLENDED SUBMISSIONS\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"\\n--- Generating Blended Submissions ---\")\n\n# Blend 1: LGBM (50%) + XGBoost (50%)\nprint(\"Creating 50-50 Blend...\")\nblend_5050_log = (0.5 * lgb_test_preds) + (0.5 * xgb_test_preds)\nblend_5050_raw = np.expm1(blend_5050_log).clip(0, None)\n#pd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': blend_5050_raw}).to_csv('submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_50_1.csv', index=False)\n#print(\"Saved: submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_50.csv\")\n\n# Blend 2: LGBM (40%) + XGBoost (60%)\nprint(\"\\nCreating 40-60 Blend (Heavier on XGBoost)...\")\nblend_4060_log = (0.4 * lgb_test_preds) + (0.6 * xgb_test_preds)\nblend_4060_raw = np.expm1(blend_4060_log).clip(0, None)\n#pd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': blend_4060_raw}).to_csv('submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_4060.csv', index=False)\n#print(\"Saved: submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_4060.csv\")\n\n# Blend 3: LGBM (60%) + XGBoost (40%)\nprint(\"\\nCreating 60-40 Blend (Heavier on LightGBM)...\")\nblend_6040_log = (0.6 * lgb_test_preds) + (0.4 * xgb_test_preds)\nblend_6040_raw = np.expm1(blend_6040_log).clip(0, None)\npd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': blend_6040_raw}).to_csv('submission', index=False)\nprint(\"Saved: submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_6040.csv\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.2 Log-Space Ensemble Blending & Final Submission Generation\n\nThis section combines predictions from our optimal **LightGBM** and **XGBoost** models into weighted ensemble blends, applies inverse target transformations, and formats final CSV submission files.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Code Execution Workflow\n\n1. **Log-Space Weighted Averaging**:\n   Combines predictions directly in log space before exponentiating. Averaging in log space is mathematically equivalent to computing a **weighted geometric mean** in original dollar values:\n   $$\\hat{y}_{\\text{blend, log}} = w_{\\text{lgb}} \\cdot \\hat{y}_{\\text{lgb, log}} + w_{\\text{xgb}} \\cdot \\hat{y}_{\\text{xgb, log}}$$\n2. **Inverse Log Transformation (`np.expm1`)**:\n   Converts log-predictions back to raw currency values using the exponential minus 1 transform:\n   $$\\hat{y}_{\\text{raw}} = \\exp(\\hat{y}_{\\text{blend, log}}) - 1$$\n3. **Non-Negative Value Clipping (`.clip(0, None)`)**:\n   Enforces lower boundary constraints ($\\text{Price} \\ge 0$), preventing negative price predictions.\n4. **CSV Export**:\n   Formats `TransactionID` and predicted `TargetValue` into final Kaggle submission files.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Ensemble Strategy & Mathematical Rationale\n\n### **Why Blend in Log Space?**\nSince our competition evaluation metric is **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)**:\n$$\\text{RMSLE} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( \\ln(\\hat{y}_i + 1) - \\ln(y_i + 1) \\right)^2}$$\n\nAveraging model predictions in log space minimizes the exact squared logarithmic error function directly. Averaging raw dollar predictions instead would result in sub-optimal RMSLE scores.\n\n### **Ensemble Blend Configurations Tested**\n\n| Ensemble Blend | Weights ($w_{\\text{lgb}} : w_{\\text{xgb}}$) | Mathematical Property | Kaggle Public RMSLE |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :---: |\n| **Equal Blend** | $50\\% : 50\\%$ | Symmetric variance reduction | 0.19740 |\n| **XGBoost-Heavy** | $40\\% : 60\\%$ | Favors level-wise tree structures | 0.19813 |\n| **LightGBM-Heavy** | **$60\\% : 40\\%$** | **Favors leaf-wise fine detail splits** | **0.19704** |\n\n> **Key Insight**: The **60-40 LightGBM-Heavy blend** yielded the top Kaggle public score (**0.19704**) because LightGBM's leaf-wise tree growth captured non-linear price boundaries slightly better on high-volume asset classes, while XGBoost provided regularization stability.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n\n'''\nn_estimators=1200,\n    subsample=0.8,\n    colsample_bytree=0.7,\n    random_state=42,\n    verbose=-1,\n    n_jobs=-1,\n    **best_lgb_params\n\n\n\nn_estimators=1000,\n    colsample_bytree=0.7,\n    tree_method='hist',\n    enable_categorical=True,\n    random_state=42,\n    verbosity=0,\n    n_jobs=-1,\n    **best_xgb_params\n)\n\n'''\n\n# TRAIN_TEST SPILT with grind cv\n# 0.19748 50-50\n#0.19707   60-40\n# lgbm 0.19805\n# 0.19813 40-60\n\n#TRAIN_TEST SPILT with random cv\n# 0.19740    50-50  \n##0.19704   60-40\n# CRONOGICAL SPILT\n\n#cornogical  with griend cv\n# 0.19708 50-50\n#cornogical  with randomcv \n\n# 0.19729 50-50 \n# 0.19790 60-40 \n\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 🎯 Overfitting vs. Underfitting: Core Concepts & Playbook\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Think of machine learning models like students preparing for an exam:\n> * **Underfitting** is the student who barely studied. They didn't capture the core concepts, so they perform poorly on both practice tests and the final exam.\n> * **Overfitting** is the student who memorized the practice questions word for word. They get a perfect score on the practice tests, but if the real exam changes even a single word, they fail completely because they didn't learn the underlying patterns.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Definitions & The Bias-Variance Tradeoff\n\nTo understand why these occur, we evaluate two foundational forces in Machine Learning: **Bias** (Rigidity) and **Variance** (Flexibility).\n\n### 🐢 Underfitting (High Bias, Low Variance)\n* **What it is:** The model is too simple to capture the underlying structure of the data.\n* **The Cause (High Bias):** The model makes strong, rigid assumptions about the data (e.g., attempting to fit a straight line through data that follows a complex curve).\n* **The Result:** Poor performance on both the training data and new validation data.\n\n### 🐒 Overfitting (Low Bias, High Variance)\n* **What it is:** The model learns both the underlying genuine patterns and the random noise/flukes in the training dataset.\n* **The Cause (High Variance):** The model is overly flexible and hyper-sensitive to small fluctuations in the training set.\n* **The Result:** Near-perfect performance on training data, but poor generalization on new validation data.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Model Sensitivity & Risk Factors\n\n| Condition / Factor | Underfitting Risk (High Bias) | Overfitting Risk (High Variance) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Dataset Size** | N/A | **Small Datasets:** High risk of memorizing individual rows. |\n| **Feature-to-Row Ratio** | N/A | **High Dimensions:** (e.g., 500 columns vs. 1,000 rows) leads to coincidental correlations. |\n| **Model Complexity** | **Oversimplified Models:** Linear models on non-linear spatial data. | **Excessive Complexity:** Deep trees (`max_depth=15`) with 1,000+ estimators. |\n| **Regularization** | **Heavy Constraints:** Penalties set so tight the model cannot learn. | **Unconstrained:** Zero regularization allows uncontrolled growth. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Prevention & Remediation Playbook\n\n[Underfitting / High Bias]  <======  O P T I M A L  ======>  [Overfitting / High Variance]\n    (Model Too Simple)                                           (Model Too Complex)\n\n\n### 🔧 How to Fix Underfitting (Increase Model Capacity)\n1. **Increase Model Complexity:** Give the model more breathing room (e.g., increase `max_depth` or `num_leaves`).\n2. **Feature Engineering:** Add relevant features, interaction terms (`FuncClass_x_InvGroup`), or historical aggregates.\n3. **Reduce Regularization / Train Longer:** Lower L2 penalties (`l2_leaf_reg`) or allow boosting algorithms to run for more iterations (`n_estimators`).\n\n---\n\n### 🛡️ How to Fix Overfitting (Control & Generalize)\n1. **Cross-Validation / Validation Splits:** Use `PredefinedSplit` or K-Fold CV to ensure you never evaluate on data the model has already seen during training.\n2. **Regularization Penalties:** Introduce explicit penalties for structural complexity:\n   * **CatBoost:** `l2_leaf_reg`\n   * **LightGBM / XGBoost:** `reg_alpha` (L1) and `reg_lambda` (L2)\n3. **Subsampling (Feature & Row Masking):**\n   * `subsample=0.7`: Forces each tree to train on a random $70\\%$ subset of rows.\n   * `colsample_bytree=0.6`: Forces each tree to train on a random $60\\%$ subset of features.\n4. **Early Stopping:** Automatically halt training when validation error stops improving, preventing the model from learning training noise.\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 🎛️ Understanding Learning Rate & Tree Balancing\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Learning Rate ($\\eta$ / `learning_rate`):** Controls how aggressively each new tree in Gradient Boosting corrects the mistakes of previous trees.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Comparing Learning Rates\n\n* **High Learning Rate ($\\eta = 0.7$ — Too High):** \n  The first few trees make massive, aggressive adjustments. While it learns quickly, the model overshoots the optimal target, oscillates wildly, and rapidly overfits the training data. It loses the ability to capture subtle, underlying patterns.\n\n* **Optimal Learning Rate ($\\eta = 0.05$ — Just Right):** \n  Each tree contributes a small, controlled amount ($5\\%$) toward the final correction. Subsequent trees gently smooth out remaining residual errors. While it requires more trees, the resulting model is significantly more accurate, robust, and stable.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Balancing Act: Learning Rate vs. `n_estimators`\n\nLearning rate never works in isolation—it operates in direct partnership with `n_estimators` (total tree count). \n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **The Boat Steering Analogy:**\n> * **A $0.7$ Learning Rate** is like yanking the steering wheel hard left and right. You turn fast, but you risk crashing or overcorrecting. You only need a few trees before the model breaks.\n> * **A $0.05$ Learning Rate** is like making small, precise micro-adjustments to the steering wheel. Because each adjustment is small, you need more total turns (`n_estimators = 1000`) to reach your destination safely.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Executive Comparison Matrix\n\n| Feature / Metric | Low Learning Rate (`0.01` – `0.05`) | High Learning Rate (`0.3` – `0.7`) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Step Size** | Tiny, incremental steps | Massive, aggressive jumps |\n| **Required Trees (`n_estimators`)** | High (e.g., $800$ to $1500+$ trees) | Very low (e.g., $20$ to $50$ trees) |\n| **Overfitting Risk** | Low (when paired with Early Stopping) | Extremely High |\n| **Final Generalization** | **Excellent** (smooth decision boundaries) | **Poor** (jagged, hyper-specific rules) |\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# MILESTONE 1","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"'''\n# --------------- 1. LOAD DATA --------------------------------\ntrain = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/competitions/heavy-equipment-selling-price-prediction-challenge/train.csv', low_memory=False)\ntest  = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/competitions/heavy-equipment-selling-price-prediction-challenge/test.csv',  low_memory=False)\n\n\n#---------------questions ----------------\nprint(\"Q-1\")\nprint(train.shape,test.shape)\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint('Q-2')\nprint(set(train.columns) - set(test.columns))\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(train.info())\nprint(\" q-3 there are 6 colums\")\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-4\")\nprint((train.isnull().mean()*100).sort_values(ascending=False))\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-5\")\nprint(train['OperationalHoursMeter'].isnull().mean() * 100)\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-6\")\nprint(train['TargetValue'].median())\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-7\")\nprint(train['ManufactureYear'].unique())\nprint(train['ManufactureYear'].mode()[0])\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-8\")\nprint(train['TransactionDate'].nunique())\nprint(train['TransactionDate'].min())\n\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\n\nprint(\"Q-9\")\nprint(\"Unique classes in Spec_BaseClass:\")\nprint(train['Spec_BaseClass'].nun